WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.615
You write two SQL queries that look different.

00:00:05.615 --> 00:00:09.229
Both run, and both return the same answer on your test data.

00:00:09.229 --> 00:00:12.844
Are they equivalent?

00:00:12.844 --> 00:00:16.458
Maybe.

00:00:16.458 --> 00:00:20.073
There is a sharp line here.

00:00:20.073 --> 00:00:23.687
Output equivalence, on the right, means two queries agree on the data you have right now.

00:00:23.687 --> 00:00:27.302
Like rating greater than four point five versus rating at least four point six.

00:00:27.302 --> 00:00:30.916
They match today, but the moment a row rates four point five five, they split.

00:00:30.916 --> 00:00:34.531
That is a coincidence.

00:00:34.531 --> 00:00:38.146
Query equivalence, on the left, means two queries agree on any input, ever.

00:00:38.146 --> 00:00:41.760
Like x between one and five versus x at least one and at most five.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.375
Always the same.

00:00:45.375 --> 00:00:48.989
That is a guarantee.

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:52.604
And here is the catch you cannot test your way out of.

00:00:52.604 --> 00:00:56.218
Running both queries and seeing the same rows only proves they agree on the rows you have.

00:00:56.218 --> 00:00:59.833
It never proves they agree on the rows you do not.

00:00:59.833 --> 00:01:03.447
For that you have to reason about what each one actually computes.

00:01:03.447 --> 00:01:07.062
Let's do that for three examples.

00:01:07.062 --> 00:01:11.454
Example one.

00:01:11.454 --> 00:01:15.847
Find every user with at least one rating above their own personal average.

00:01:15.847 --> 00:01:20.239
Both queries compute the very same value, this user's average rating, and both keep a user when some rating beats it.

00:01:20.239 --> 00:01:24.632
They differ only in when they compute it.

00:01:24.632 --> 00:01:29.024
The correlated subquery re-derives the average on every single row, from scratch.

00:01:29.024 --> 00:01:33.416
The CTE derives it once, up front, and joins it back.

00:01:33.416 --> 00:01:37.809
But a user's average is a fixed number.

00:01:37.809 --> 00:01:42.201
Compute it once or compute it per row, you get the same number.

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:46.594
And the test, rating above that average, is identical.

00:01:46.594 --> 00:01:50.986
Same value in, same comparison, same users out, for absolutely any data you could load.

00:01:50.986 --> 00:01:55.379
So these two are genuinely query-equivalent.

00:01:55.379 --> 00:01:59.771
Here they return Mickey, Minnie, Daffy.

00:01:59.771 --> 00:02:03.609
Example two.

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:07.446
Find users who span at least two genres.

00:02:07.446 --> 00:02:11.284
Another genuine equivalence, and the comments show why.

00:02:11.284 --> 00:02:15.122
Both join listens to songs, count the distinct genres for each user, and keep the users with two or more.

00:02:15.122 --> 00:02:18.959
The CTE version computes nothing new.

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:22.797
It just gives that distinct-genre count a name, genre count, and filters on the name one step later.

00:02:22.797 --> 00:02:26.634
Naming a value cannot change the value.

00:02:26.634 --> 00:02:30.472
Same count, same threshold, same answer, on every possible input.

00:02:30.472 --> 00:02:34.310
They return Mickey, Minnie, Daffy.

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:38.147
So that is two genuine equivalences.

00:02:38.147 --> 00:02:41.985
Now watch one that only looks like a third.

00:02:41.985 --> 00:02:46.682
Example three.

00:02:46.682 --> 00:02:51.378
Each user's top three songs.

00:02:51.378 --> 00:02:56.075
Both queries ask the same question, where does a song rank inside its own user.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:03:00.771
The window version ranks directly.

00:03:00.771 --> 00:03:05.468
ROW_NUMBER, ordered by rating within each user, stamps one, two, three, and you keep rank three or lower.

00:03:05.468 --> 00:03:10.165
The classical version never ranks.

00:03:10.165 --> 00:03:14.861
For each song it counts how many of that user's songs rate strictly higher, and keeps the song when fewer than three beat it.

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:19.558
On distinct ratings those are the same statement: the song in position three is exactly the song with two above it.

00:03:19.558 --> 00:03:24.254
So on our data they agree.

00:03:24.254 --> 00:03:28.951
But watch one extra tuple.

00:03:28.951 --> 00:03:33.648
Give Mickey a second song rated three point nine, tying for third.

00:03:33.648 --> 00:03:38.344
ROW_NUMBER hands out one, two, three, four, so that fourth song is rank four, and it gets dropped: three songs.

00:03:38.344 --> 00:03:43.041
But that same song still has only two rated higher, fewer than three, so the counting version keeps it: four songs.

00:03:43.041 --> 00:03:47.737
The tie at the cutoff is exactly where they split.

00:03:47.737 --> 00:03:52.434
Equal output today, not equal queries.

00:03:52.434 --> 00:03:56.841
So where do these look-alikes actually split?

00:03:56.841 --> 00:04:01.248
Two places, and neither shows up in our nine rows.

00:04:01.248 --> 00:04:05.655
First, the tie, from example three.

00:04:05.655 --> 00:04:10.062
Give one user four songs all tied at five point oh.

00:04:10.062 --> 00:04:14.468
ROW_NUMBER forces a strict order and cuts at three, so it keeps three songs.

00:04:14.468 --> 00:04:18.875
The counting version sees zero songs rated higher for all four, so it keeps all four.

00:04:18.875 --> 00:04:23.282
Same intent, different tie-handling, and nothing forbids a tie.

00:04:23.282 --> 00:04:27.689
Second, a trap that hits a pattern you will reach for constantly, NOT IN.

00:04:27.689 --> 00:04:32.096
Picture its exclude-list holding a two and a null.

00:04:32.096 --> 00:04:36.503
NOT IN asks, is this user different from every value in the list?

00:04:36.503 --> 00:04:40.910
You are comparing a known id to an unknown, so SQL answers unknown, and the row drops.

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:45.317
The whole result collapses to nothing.

00:04:45.317 --> 00:04:49.724
NOT EXISTS asks a plain existence question that a null cannot poison.

00:04:49.724 --> 00:04:54.131
Now the subtle part.

00:04:54.131 --> 00:04:58.537
On our schema this never arises, because Listens dot user id is declared NOT NULL.

00:04:58.537 --> 00:05:02.944
And that is the NOT NULL constraint doing the work, not the foreign key.

00:05:02.944 --> 00:05:07.351
A foreign key still allows nulls.

00:05:07.351 --> 00:05:11.758
It only checks that present values point at a real user.

00:05:11.758 --> 00:05:16.165
So the same NOT IN query is safe here, and a landmine the day you aim it at a column that can be null.

00:05:16.165 --> 00:05:20.456
A few rules for picking.

00:05:20.456 --> 00:05:24.747
EXISTS over IN when NULLs are possible, because we just saw IN break on a null.

00:05:24.747 --> 00:05:29.038
Window functions over correlated subqueries when the database optimizer can rewrite them.

00:05:29.038 --> 00:05:33.329
CTE over a deeply nested subquery for readability.

00:05:33.329 --> 00:05:37.620
Each pick has a reason rooted in equivalence.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.125
So here is the model to keep.

00:05:42.125 --> 00:05:46.629
Two queries that look the same and return the same answer today are not necessarily the same query.

00:05:46.629 --> 00:05:51.134
Real equivalence is about every future input.

00:05:51.134 --> 00:05:55.639
The edge cases, the ties, the nulls.

00:05:55.639 --> 00:06:00.144
That is where look-alike queries split apart, exactly as the tie and the null just did.

00:06:00.144 --> 00:06:04.648
And remember what a test can and cannot do.

00:06:04.648 --> 00:06:09.153
Running the queries shows they agree on the rows you have.

00:06:09.153 --> 00:06:13.658
Only reasoning about what they compute shows they agree on the rows you do not.

00:06:13.658 --> 00:06:18.162
Get that right, and you pick the shape that reads clearest and trust the optimizer for speed.

00:06:18.162 --> 00:06:22.667
Get it wrong, and you ship a bug that only shows up on tomorrow's data.
